A1. Irish picnics. ~ SCRAPS
A6. Preposition. ~ AT
A8. These, like wives, never have anything to wear; they're always up in the air and are continually harping about something. ~ ANGELS
A13. What barbers use when they cut people's chins. ~ ALUM
A14. An abbreviated business concern. ~ CO
A15. A muffler should always be worn with this kind of necktie. ~ LOUD
A16. Preposition. ~ OF
A18. What the big gun in an office gets along on until he gets fired. ~ PRETENSION
A21. Pronoun. ~ ME
A22. The dangerous age. ~ OLD
A24. Place where a cauliflower sometimes blossoms. ~ EAR
A25. This is shoved but a pencil must be lead. ~ PEN
A26. Product of a boiler factory. ~ DIN
A27. What borrowing $10 from a Scotchman is. ~ LOAN
A29. This comes from a bolt. (Not lightning). ~ RIBBON
A32. Something modern children never do. ~ MIND
A33. What the spies did in the World War. ~ SPIED
A35. A big ditch. ~ MOAT
A36. Mosquitoes, book agents and crossword puzzle fans. ~ PESTS
A37. A way to drink whisky. ~ NEAT
A39. Where the clouds roll. ~ BY
A40. This has but one leg. ~ PANT
A41. A 1925 mamma. ~ HOT
A43. Don't go out in a rowboat unless you can do this. ~ ROW
A45. To do the wrong thing at the wrong time. ~ ERR
A46. Total of one and one in the school of love. (If there is such a school!) ~ ONE
A49. Something the Swiss do besides make cheese and yodel. ~ SKI
A50. Weeping and wailing and gnashing of teeth. ~ WOE
A51. Interjection of an inebriate at a country fair. ~ HIC
A52. The speech of an opponent of a political candidate. ~ ROT
A53. Something that should be hit on the head. ~ NAIL
A55. Afternoon (abbr.). ~ PM
A57. Something that even the hardest of hearts do. ~ MELT
A59. This kind of parent is passé. ~ STERN
A61. Something all good cannons do. ~ BOOM
A63. Hiding places for lions and landlords. ~ LAIRS
A66. A guy who throws a rough party. (Not a victorious wrestler.) ~ HOST
A67. Substance. ~ MATTER
A69. This throws a light on many a love affair. ~ MOON
A70. An obstinate animal. ~ ASS
A71. The delight of Aunty Everything. ~ BAN
A72. Sparkplug. ~ NAG
A74. A New Yorker's "new." ~ NOO
A75. An afterthought in a letter (abbr.). ~ PS
A76. The way flappers would like to dress. (Careful, now!) ~ GORGEOUSLY
A80. The "show me State" (abbr.). ~ MO
A81. People who work in one of these can't take home samples. ~ BANK
A82. A conjunction. ~ OR
A83. This makes a good slip on. ~ PEEL
A85. Scrubwomen aren't the only people who go under a table nowadays, these do too. ~ SOUSES
A86. Note well (abbr.). ~ NB
A87. A spoonholder. ~ SETTEE

